# One program asked, as the compiler asks clang, and what it said read back
# whole: the verdict of a run, `ok` with its words or `fail` with the
# refusal's (design.md §4.17; CLAUDE.md §7, a clang failure being the
# compiler's own unless a named class says the author's). Split from
# `cli/toolchain.hero` on 2026-10-11 (defect 644), past §11's 300 lines,
# along the seam between finding the toolchain and asking it; `toolchain`'s
# `asked_of_clang`, `asked_through` and `run_clang` are its callers, and
# `cli/link_warning.hero` asks the link through it with stdout read.

use cli/process
use cli/read_back
use cli/strings
use cli/clang_died

# One program's line, its first word the program's own name as a line's is
# (`hero_run_go`), its stderr written to `err_path` and read back; and where
# `out_path` names a file, what the program wrote on stdout read before its
# stderr when it fails: **MSVC's link.exe writes its
# refusals on stdout** (defect 644, measured on the Windows box 2026-10-11
# with clang 20.1.8, the CI's: *error LNK2019: unresolved external symbol*
# went nowhere and the failure said only *linker command failed with exit
# code 1120*). A link that succeeds is read as it was, its stdout left
# unsaid.
function asked(program: str, words: [str], what: str, err_path: str, out_path: str) -> str?
    # **`discard()` and not `""`, because the empty path INHERITS.** Under the
    # net this compiler is a child whose stdout is a capture file, and a clang
    # that outlives it goes on holding that file: `docs/panel/174` route F, and
    # defect 074 is what it cost.
    out: str @= process.discard()

    if out_path.len() > 0
        out @ out_path
    ran = process.run(program: program, words: words, out: out, err: err_path)
    noise: str @= ""
    written: str @= ""

    if out_path.len() > 0
        said_out = read_back.words(out_path)

        if !said_out.is_err()
            written @ said_out.must()

    # **clang's words are read back where the bytes enter**, by
    # `read_back.words`, so every reader after it has the two repairs it
    # makes. A byte that is not UTF-8 is written by its value (defect 337):
    # clang prints a location's file name as it was given, and `read_file`
    # refused the whole stream for one such byte, so an author's extern
    # disagreement was `internal error` over an empty line at exit 2. And a
    # line ended `\r\n` is read as ended `\n`, because **clang on Windows ends
    # its lines `\r\n`, and the `\r` is poison downstream**: the MSVC-built
    # clang.exe writes text-mode line endings, so every line of its stderr
    # carried a trailing `\r` into `emit_ffi`'s parsing: the last word of
    # `heroes-ffi-return sqrt i64` became `i64\r`, the diagnostic quoted
    # `` `i64\r` `` (measured 2026-08-31, `od` on the captured file: `i 6 4 \r`
    # inside the message), and `heroes-ffi-const`'s marker stopped matching at
    # all, which demoted the author's own error to exit 2.
    captured = read_back.words(err_path)

    if !captured.is_err()
        noise @ captured.must()

    # **Never started is not the same as ran and failed**, and until
    # M-argv-execution this line could not tell them apart: `system()`
    # answers 127 for a missing program and for one that exits 127
    # alike, so a machine with no clang was reported as the compiler's
    # own bug — *"internal error: the runtime did not compile"*.
    # **AND "NOT STARTED" IS NOT THE SAME AS "NOT THERE."** This line named the
    # PATH for every refusal the operating system makes, including one about a
    # file a descendant held, on a machine whose clang had never moved. The
    # number separates them (`docs/panel/174` route G).
    # **Its own code, too** (defect 134): it said `clang`, the code of a
    # refusal, so the caller worded a clang that never ran as clang refusing
    # the generated C. `clang` below means clang ran and exited non-zero.
    if !ran.started
        return fail(
            code: "not_started",
            msg: "clang could not be started, so " + what + " did not run; the operating system's own reason is " + ran.why.to_str() + ".\n  If it is 2 the program is not on this machine's PATH and `heroes doctor` says what is; any other number is the machine refusing for its own reason"
        )

    if ran.code == 0
        return ok(noise)
    noise @ written + noise
    died: str @= clang_died.died_by(noise)

    # **A C COMPILER THAT DIED REFUSED NOTHING** (defect 170, 2026-10-03), and
    # it says so in one of two ways: Apple clang 21 catches its own compiler's
    # death and its driver says *failed due to signal*; Debian clang 22.1.8 on
    # Linux arm64 died whole, by the signal itself, and wrote nothing. Either
    # way the C was never judged, which is neither this compiler's mistake nor
    # the author's, so it is neither `internal error` nor clang's crash report,
    # whose files and backtrace speak to clang's own developers. Said here,
    # once, in this compiler's words.
    if died.len() == 0 && ran.signal != 0
        died @ clang_died.ending(ran.signal)

    if died.len() > 0
        return fail(
            code: "crashed",
            msg: what + " stopped: clang died on the C instead of judging it (" + died + "), so nothing in it was refused"
        )
    return fail(code: "clang", msg: refusal(what: what, code: ran.code, noise: noise))

# What clang's refusal says: its own words under what failed, or where it
# wrote none, that it said nothing and how it exited (defect 326's shape),
# where `linking failed:` stood over an empty line.
function refusal(what: str, code: i64, noise: str) -> str
    if strings.trimmed(noise).len() == 0
        return what + " failed: clang exited " + code.to_str() + " and said nothing"
    return what + " failed:\n" + noise

## Tests

# Defect 326's shape at clang: a refusal with no word in it, or only line ends,
# says so and how clang exited, where `linking failed:` stood over an empty
# line; one with words carries them under what failed, as it did.
test "a clang that refused and said nothing is told so, never as an empty line (defect 326)"
    assert refusal(what: "linking", code: 1, noise: "") == "linking failed: clang exited 1 and said nothing"
    assert refusal(what: "linking", code: 1, noise: "\n\n") == "linking failed: clang exited 1 and said nothing"
    assert refusal(what: "linking", code: 1, noise: "ld: symbol(s) not found\n") == "linking failed:\nld: symbol(s) not found\n"
